## Section Endings

Every homepage section that ends with a "view all" link (All projects) follows one pattern, so the link reads as part of the section above it:

| Element | Classes |
|---------|---------|
| Link wrapper | `mt-12 flex justify-center border-b border-border pb-8` |
| Link | `text-link inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium tracking-wide text-foreground uppercase` |

The link sits directly under the content, and the divider line **closes** the section. Never put the divider above the link.

## Homepage Order

About (Oct 2026, staging: the /about page was folded into the homepage and now opens it, `components/homepage/about-intro.tsx`; the portrait circle with the gold line drawn across it, the page's h1, then the story along a gold spine with brush-and-ink drawings, "What I do", and the closing role line; the spine turns to the centre and ends in an ink drop) → Hero (eyebrow label, two-line headline as an h2, three number stats with hairline dividers) → black client band → Case Studies (+ Project Highlights; the section closes on its divider because /projects is hidden on staging and 301s to /#work) → white services marquee → Testimonials + Stats, which is the last section before the footer and keeps the bottom padding (`pb-24 lg:pb-32`). /about 301s to /#about. The separate homepage CTA was removed in Oct 2026; the footer carries the contact prompt. The skills marquee was retired in Oct 2026. Articles were retired in Oct 2026 (/articles 301s to /). Page metadata goes through `pageMeta()` in `lib/seo.ts`.


## Homepage Section Rhythm

Each homepage section owns its **top** spacing only: `pt-32 lg:pt-48`, and the services marquee uses `mt-32 lg:mt-48`. It ends at its divider line or CTA with no bottom padding, so the gap between any two sections is the same: 128px on mobile, 192px on desktop. Only the last section before the footer keeps bottom padding.

## CSS Baseline (globals.css)

These apply globally and must not be overridden:
- `font-kerning: normal`: optical pair kerning.
- `font-feature-settings: 'liga' 1, 'calt' 1`: ligatures and contextual alternates.
- `text-wrap: pretty` on body, p, li, blockquote, td, th, figcaption.
- `text-wrap: balance` and `overflow-wrap: break-word` on h1–h6.
- `hyphens: manual` on body: words are never split. Line breaks come from the typesetting rules.

## Accessibility floor

- Text is at least 4.5:1 on its ground (3:1 at 24px and up).
- Control boundaries and focus indicators are at least 3:1.
- The focus ring is 2px in `--accent-text`, offset 3px, on light grounds (including the white footer card), and `--accent` on ink.
- Form fields: an `--input-strong` underline at rest, and a 2px `--accent-text` underline on focus.

## Positioning (Oct 2026)

The site is written for hiring managers first: one fact sheet everywhere (13 years, teams of up to 25, 8 markets, 50+ people hired), UK English, no named current employer. Consulting and Working Together were retired in Oct 2026 (/consulting, /work-with-me and /working-together redirect to /contact). Contract and interim work is one short note on /contact, so the site reads as a hire, not a freelance offer. LinkedIn sits in the main nav as the fast contact route. Case-study CTAs lead with “Hiring a Creative Director?”.

## Voice (Oct 2026)

Copy is written in Amr's voice, built from his own words. Rules:
- Short declarative sentences, first person, owning the work. Plain words.
- A story before a number. At most one or two numbers per paragraph.
- Grounded, senior tone that still flows: long-form paragraphs that tell a story (situation, what he did, what changed), varied sentence length, no mini subheadings inside a bio. His art background is illustration, calligraphy and typography (never 'drawing and sculpture').
- Write for the hiring manager: their problem first, then the proof he solved it before.
- Use his phrases verbatim where they fit: "Skills can be sharpened. Anything else can be fixed." "From brief to a live staging build in days, not sprints."
- Banned: arrow lists (→), "in practice", colon reveals, labels-as-sentences ("How I lead:"), runs of three-item lists, "not just X but Y", semicolons, filler verbs (leverage, drive, elevate).
- The "Open to roles in Europe" line appears only in the hero, the About close and the contact page.
- Test: would he say this sentence out loud in an interview?
- Register is a senior creative director's, read by boards, CEOs and other CDs at large agencies and companies, not only hiring managers. Decision first, then the evidence. Judgement and outcomes over activity. No chatty openers ("X came to me...").
- Every strategic call states its why (e.g. SMB positioning: existing reach through word of mouth and the CEO's network, plus existing work and case studies for that market).
- Former employers' leaders are "the CEO", never "the owner". Never mention how or why a role ended. No lines that undercut the work ("not launched", "redrawn for this case study").

## Footer (Oct 2026)

Option B from the footer lab (`footer-options` branch, /footer-lab): a white form card hanging from the top edge (7 of 12 columns) beside the "Let’s talk" words. Name and email side by side, topic as one-tap pills, focus is a 2px `--accent-text` underline over an `--input-strong` resting underline (never a box or ring), inputs carry `autoComplete`. Messages go through Web3Forms (`lib/contact.ts`) with the topic in the subject and the sender as reply-to. On /contact the card is dropped because the page has its own form, and the homepage uses the same card-free footer (Oct 2026, staging).

## Buttons (Oct 2026)

Every CTA uses one of two classes from `globals.css`, never hand-built pill classes:
- `btn btn-primary`: filled ink (`--primary`, the same colour as the footer). One per view, for the main action.
- `btn btn-secondary`: outlined ink. For the alternative action next to a primary (e.g. "Book a 30-min call" beside "Send message").
States are built in: hover lifts 2px (primary lightens, secondary gets a 6% ink wash), active presses to 98%, focus-visible shows a 2px `--accent-text` ring (`--accent` on ink), disabled fades to 50%. Buttons are at least 44px tall. Reduced motion removes the movement. Add `cta-arrow` to an arrow icon to nudge it on hover.
- `btn-sm`: add to either class for the compact nav size (40px tall). The nav "Let’s talk" is `btn btn-secondary btn-sm`.

## Typesetting safety net (Oct 2026)

`tp()` now also binds articles and common short words (the, and, for, our, with…) and splits any bound chain longer than 18 characters from the right, so headings never overflow and the last line never holds one word. `components/typeset-dom.tsx` runs `tp()` over all page text after render, so strings that were not wrapped still follow the rules. Add `data-no-typeset` to opt an element out.

## Count-ups (Oct 2026)

Stat numbers use `components/count-up.tsx`. It counts every number in the value ("50+", "2–3") each time it comes into view and resets once off screen. Reduced motion shows the final value.

## Page containers (Oct 2026)

Two widths, used on every page (`globals.css`):
- `.wrap` (or `px-8 lg:px-16` + inner `mx-auto max-w-6xl`): the homepage's centred column. All text, headings, grids and cards sit in it, on every page, so the side margins match the homepage About and Leadership sections.
- `.wrap-wide`: the nav's 64px gutter only. Reserved for full-width media (case-study hero video, figures, galleries) and the homepage case-study cards.

## Case-study top (Oct 2026)

Title, subtitle, hero media, then the project facts as one horizontal band (three columns, two rows, hairline above), then an Overview on the chapter grid (label left, intro right, first paragraph large). "What I led" follows as its own section on the same grid (label left, two-column list with serif titles right). Case studies without an intro show the impact statement there instead.

## Homepage About balance (Oct 2026)

The portrait and the text are centred as a group (`lg:items-center lg:justify-center`, portrait 38% capped at 420px, text max 36rem) so the round portrait doesn't pull the section to the left.

## Detail-page facts strip and highlight overview (Oct 2026)

The facts strip under a hero spans the hero width (hairline edge to edge) with its items grouped and centred (`lg:flex lg:justify-center`), so the content has equal margins left and right, with the items spread edge to edge (equal columns, one per fact), hairline above, on case studies and highlights alike. Highlights open with a two-column Overview: label and the first paragraph as a serif lead on the left, the remaining paragraphs on the right, so no column is left empty.
